# CORE

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Core section gathers exercises that develop trunk strength, stability, force transmission and movement efficiency.

Within CAS, the core is not viewed as a group of abdominal muscles but as the central system responsible for transferring force between the upper and lower body.

---

# Philosophy

Combat athletes do not require visible abdominal muscles.

They require a trunk capable of producing, resisting and transferring force in every direction while maintaining optimal posture under fatigue.

CAS develops the core as a performance system rather than an aesthetic objective.

---

# Definition

The core includes every structure responsible for stabilizing and coordinating the trunk and pelvis.

It includes, but is not limited to:

- Rectus Abdominis
- Internal Oblique
- External Oblique
- Transverse Abdominis
- Erector Spinae
- Multifidus
- Quadratus Lumborum
- Diaphragm
- Pelvic Floor
- Hip Stabilizers

---

# Objectives

Core training develops:

- Force transmission
- Anti-extension capacity
- Anti-rotation capacity
- Anti-lateral flexion
- Dynamic trunk stability
- Rotational power
- Movement efficiency
- Injury resilience

---

# Why It Matters For Combat Sports

Every combat action passes through the trunk.

Examples include:

- Punching
- Kicking
- Clinching
- Throwing
- Takedowns
- Ground transitions
- Escapes
- Carrying
- Bracing under impact

A stronger core improves performance by allowing force generated by the legs to be transferred efficiently to the upper body.

